  "strategy": "To minimize the evaluation function J(a) = 2*n*max(conv(a,a))/ (sum(a))^2, we use a scaled uniform initialization strategy with adaptive subgradient optimization:\n\n1. Approach and rationale\n- The central idea is to search for sequences that maintain high autocorrelation contrast at scale boundaries, enabling better optimization\n- We use automatic differentiation to compute scaling factors that expose non-linearities in the evaluation function\n- The Fourier Transform provides a computationally efficient way to explore high-dimensional search spaces\n- Our solution combines iterative scaling and analytical gradient computation to reduce the automatic maximum\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe evaluation function is J(a) = 2*n*max_{k}(convolve(a,a,k)) / (sum(a))^2, where:\n- a \u2208 \u211d^+\u2099 (non-negative real numbers)\n- k \u2208 {0,1,...,2n-2}\n- convolve(a,a,k) = \u2211_{i+j=k} a[i]*a[j] (full convolution)\n\nFor any point x in \u211d^n, let:\n- f(x) = max_k convolve(x,k)\n- s(x) = sum(x)\nThen J(x) = (2*n*f(x)) / (s(x))^2\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nSteps:\n1. Initialize random number generator and default parameters\n2. Select and validate parent sequence if provided\n3. Create scaled uniform initializations across dimensions [1,100,1000]\n4. For each iteration:\n   a. Compute uniform scaling and adjust constraints\n   b. Identify n_active (maximum active sequences) via FFT\n   c. Perform analytical subgradient descent\n   d. Update sequence using coordinate-wise gradient descent\n   e. Check for convergence by comparing Z-normalization\n5. Implement adaptive momentum for slow convergence\n6. Conduct active set balancing with sparse matrix conversion\n7. Apply advanced constraint bootstrapping using FFT and rounding\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n- Use multiple length factors and dynamically adjust dimensions\n- Implement FFT-based gradient computation for all major operations\n- Use uniformly distributed perturbations and exponential decay factors\n- Combine with active set balancing for faster convergence\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- Each candidate evaluation: compute uniform scaling and FFT convolutions\n- Time budget: set per-candidate time budget to 1/\u221a(t+1)\n- Fallback sequence: use No. 1 scaling if dimension is too large (n>4096)\n- Absolute convergence tolerance: \u221at*J_threshold with J_lowerset=1.5027\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Keep history trackers for all normalized evaluations\n- Verify fallback sequences using J(x) <= 1.5027\n- Return sequence with sum \u22650.01 and max value \u22641000\n- Automatically roll back to best previous candidate if Fourier methodologies fail.",
